course: dictionary
lesson: 6236
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «Mobile» is one of them. It can describe something that moves easily, like a «mobile crane», or it can name a phone you carry, like a «mobile phone».
explain: Ask what the sentence is about. A crane on a lorry is something that can be moved around, so mobile means able to move. If the sentence is about phones or calls, such as «Mobile phones can usually call the emergency number», mobile means the phone you carry.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. In «A mobile home or a car is not safe in a tornado», mobile means able to be moved from place to place, and that makes sense: a home that can be moved can be in danger from a storm. Read on if you are not sure, because the next words often give the clue.
fact: Mobile means able to move or be moved easily from place to place.
fact: A mobile, or mobile phone, is a phone you carry with you; in the USA it is usually called a cell phone.
fact: A mobile can also be a hanging decoration with parts that move in the air, often above a baby's cot.
fact: In science, «mobile» describes things and animals that can move.
fact: «Mobile phones can usually call the emergency number even when they are locked.»
exercise: «A mobile crane is a crane on a lorry with an arm that slides out like a telescope.» What does mobile mean in this sentence? | Quiet and still | A phone you carry with you | *Able to be moved easily from place to place | A hanging decoration above a cot
exercise: «The first mobile phones in the 1980s were as big as bricks.» What is a mobile in this sentence? | *A phone you carry with you | A hanging decoration above a cot | A small brick | A lorry with a crane on it
exercise: Ella says a mobile above a baby's cot is a phone. What is it really? | A lorry | A cot that can be moved | *A hanging decoration with parts that move in the air | A phone
test: Which meaning fits «A mobile home or a car is not safe in a tornado»? | Loud and noisy | Hanging in the air | *Able to be moved from place to place | A phone you carry with you
test: Which sentence uses mobile to mean a phone you carry? | «The animals in the science lesson are mobile.» | «A mobile crane has an arm that slides out.» | «A mobile above the cot turns slowly in the air.» | *«My mobile number is written on the card.»
exam: Jayden reads «The mobile library comes to our street on Tuesdays» and asks what mobile means. What does the context tell him? | A phone you carry with you | *It travels from place to place, so it is a library that comes to you | Something hanging in the air | Something that cannot move at all
